\import{base-macros}
\author{kellenkanarios}
\title{MSI in Murphi}
\tag{public}

\p{MESI is easier to reach through MSI, where there is less to get wrong and the races are already interesting enough.}

\section{The cache controller}{
\p{The starting point is the primer's MSI cache controller table.}
\figure{
\gridtable{
\tr{\gth{}\gth{load}\gth{store}\gth{replacement}\gth{\code{Fwd-GetS}}\gth{\code{Fwd-GetM}}\gth{\code{Inv}}\gth{\code{Put-Ack}}}
\tr{\gth{#{I}}\gtd{\code{GetS} → Dir / #{IS^D}}\gtd{\code{GetM} → Dir / #{IM^{AD}}}\gna \gna \gna \gna \gna}
\tr{\gth{#{IS^D}}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gna \gna \gtd{stall}\gna}
\tr{\gth{#{IM^{AD}}}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gna \gna}
\tr{\gth{#{IM^A}}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gna \gna}
\tr{\gth{#{S}}\gtd{hit}\gtd{\code{GetM} → Dir / #{SM^{AD}}}\gtd{\code{PutS} → Dir / #{SI^A}}\gna \gna \gtd{\code{Inv-Ack} → Req / #{I}}\gna}
\tr{\gth{#{SM^{AD}}}\gtd{hit}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gtd{\code{Inv-Ack} → Req / #{IM^{AD}}}\gna}
\tr{\gth{#{SM^A}}\gtd{hit}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gna \gna}
\tr{\gth{#{M}}\gtd{hit}\gtd{hit}\gtd{\code{PutM}+data → Dir / #{MI^A}}\gtd{data → Req + Dir / #{S}}\gtd{data → Req / #{I}}\gna \gna}
\tr{\gth{#{MI^A}}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gtd{data → Req + Dir / #{SI^A}}\gtd{data → Req / #{II^A}}\gna \gtd{\nowrap{-/#{I}}}}
\tr{\gth{#{SI^A}}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gna \gna \gtd{\code{Inv-Ack} → Req / #{II^A}}\gtd{\nowrap{-/#{I}}}}
\tr{\gth{#{II^A}}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}\gna \gna \gna \gtd{\nowrap{-/#{I}}}}
}
\figcaption{MSI cache controller, requests and forwards. Rows are states, columns are events, and each cell reads \em{action}/\em{next state}: #{\to} is "send to", a bare is "no action", an absent next state means "stay put", and a shaded cell is one the table leaves empty.}
}
\p{The response arrivals split off into their own table, because only a transient state can be waiting on one.}
\figure{
\gridtable{
\tr{\gth{}\gth{Data (ack=0)}\gth{Data (ack>0)}\gth{Data from owner}\gth{\code{Inv-Ack}}\gth{\code{Last-Inv-Ack}}}
\tr{\gth{#{IS^D}}\gtd{\nowrap{-/#{S}}}\gna \gtd{\nowrap{-/#{S}}}\gna \gna}
\tr{\gth{#{IM^{AD}}}\gtd{\nowrap{-/#{M}}}\gtd{\nowrap{-/#{IM^A}}}\gtd{\nowrap{-/#{M}}}\gtd{\nowrap{\code{ack--}}}\gna}
\tr{\gth{#{IM^A}}\gna \gna \gna \gtd{\nowrap{\code{ack--}}}\gtd{\nowrap{-/#{M}}}}
\tr{\gth{#{SM^{AD}}}\gtd{\nowrap{-/#{M}}}\gtd{\nowrap{-/#{SM^A}}}\gtd{\nowrap{-/#{M}}}\gtd{\nowrap{\code{ack--}}}\gna}
\tr{\gth{#{SM^A}}\gna \gna \gna \gtd{\nowrap{\code{ack--}}}\gtd{\nowrap{-/#{M}}}}
}
\figcaption{MSI cache controller, responses. Data comes from the directory unless the column says otherwise; the stable states are omitted, since none of them can be waiting on a response.}
}
\p{Several entries here are left blank; those are resolved at runtime from the \code{AckCount}, which is why the per-processor record carries an \code{ack} field alongside the state.}
\codeblockf{msi.m}{lua}{ProcState:
  Record
    state: enum {
      P_Invalid, P_Shared, P_Modified,   -- stable states
      PT_IS_D,     -- sent GetS, waiting for Data
      PT_IS_DI,    -- sent GetS, got Inv, waiting for Data then go to I
      PT_IM_AD,    -- sent GetM (from I), waiting for Data and InvAcks
      PT_SM_AD,    -- sent GetM (from S), waiting for Data and InvAcks
      PT_IM_A,     -- got Data for GetM, waiting for remaining InvAcks
      PT_MI_A,     -- sent PutM, waiting for PutAck
      PT_SI_A,     -- sent PutS, waiting for PutAck
      PT_II_A      -- waiting for PutAck, already invalidated
    };
    val: Value;
    ack: AckCount;  -- tracks InvAck count
  End;}
\remark{
\code{load}, \code{store}, and \code{replacement} only move the protocol between stable states. To simulate the system it is therefore enough to implement the rules that \em{initiate} those transitions evict from modified, load from invalid, and let the model checker drive the transient states from there.
}
}

\section{The directory controller}{
\p{The directory side starts from the analogous table.}
\figure{
\gridtable{
\tr{\gth{}\gth{\code{GetS}}\gth{\code{GetM}}}
\tr{\gth{#{I}}\gtd{data → Req, add Req to Sharers / #{S}}\gtd{data → Req, Owner := Req / #{M}}}
\tr{\gth{#{S}}\gtd{data → Req, add Req to Sharers}\gtd{data → Req, \code{Inv} → Sharers, clear Sharers, Owner := Req / #{M}}}
\tr{\gth{#{M}}\gtd{\code{Fwd-GetS} → Owner, add Owner + Req to Sharers, \em{clear Owner} / #{S^D}}\gtd{\code{Fwd-GetM} → Owner, Owner := Req}}
\tr{\gth{#{S^D}}\gtd{stall}\gtd{stall}}
}
\figcaption{MSI directory controller, coherence requests. The italicized \em{clear Owner} in #{M}/\code{GetS} is the table's, and is wrong (see below).}
}
\figure{
\gridtable{
\tr{\gth{}\gvth{\code{PutS}-NotLast}\gvth{\code{PutS}-Last}\gvth{\code{PutM} from Owner}\gvth{\code{PutM} from Non-Owner}\gvth{Data}}
\tr{\gth{#{I}}\gtd{\code{Put-Ack} → Req}\gtd{\code{Put-Ack} → Req}\gna \gtd{\code{Put-Ack} → Req}\gna}
\tr{\gth{#{S}}\gtd{remove Req from Sharers, \code{Put-Ack} → Req}\gtd{remove Req from Sharers, \code{Put-Ack} → Req / #{I}}\gna \gtd{remove Req from Sharers, \code{Put-Ack} → Req}\gna}
\tr{\gth{#{M}}\gtd{\code{Put-Ack} → Req}\gtd{\code{Put-Ack} → Req}\gtd{data → memory, clear Owner, \code{Put-Ack} → Req / #{I}}\gtd{\code{Put-Ack} → Req}\gna}
\tr{\gth{#{S^D}}\gtd{remove Req from Sharers, \code{Put-Ack} → Req}\gtd{remove Req from Sharers, \code{Put-Ack} → Req}\gna \gtd{remove Req from Sharers, \code{Put-Ack} → Req}\gtd{data → memory / #{S}}}
}
\figcaption{MSI directory controller, evictions and data. A \code{PutM} always carries the line with it; one from a node that is not the current owner is stale and needs only an acknowledgment.}
}
\p{This table is where the first real gap shows up. It gives the directory a single transient state, and the races below force a second one, #{M^D}.}
}

\section{Losing data to a PutM/Fwd-GetM race}{
\p{Consider the \code{PutM} rule that downgrades a cache from modified to invalid. The evicting cache must \strong{not} drop the data when it sends \code{PutM}, because it may still have to service a forwarded request; it transitions to #{MI^A} and keeps the value. But nothing stops the \code{PutAck} from arriving first, taking it to #{I} before the \code{Fwd-GetM} shows up. The data is gone and the forwarded request has nobody to answer it.}
\p{\paragraph{Fix} Add the #{M^D} state to the directory controller. While in #{M^D}, a \code{PutM} from a node that is not the current owner stalls until the data arrives. Leaving #{M^D} for #{M} requires the new owner to send data back to the directory even on a three-hop transaction, so the directory learns that the forward actually completed. I have not found a cleaner way to close this race without the extra state.}
}

\section{Deadlocking two stores against a load}{
\p{The #{M^D} fix introduces a deadlock of its own. Take two processors that store-miss and a third that load-misses. The second store wins at the directory: the first store gets data and moves #{IM^{AD} \to IM^{A}}, then the second triggers a \code{Fwd-GetM} that takes ownership away from it.}
\p{Meanwhile the load miss sits in #{IS^D}. When its \code{Inv} arrives, the table says to stall — and because \code{Inv} and \code{Fwd-GetM} share the forwarded channel, that stall blocks the \code{Fwd-GetM} behind it. The winning processor never receives the data, and nothing ever drains the channel.}
\p{\paragraph{Fix} Another transient state absent from the table: #{II^A}, meaning "was in #{IS^A}, got invalidated, now waiting on \code{PutAck} before settling in #{I}". The invalidation is processed rather than stalled, so the forwarded channel keeps moving.}
}

\section{Not clearing the owner too eagerly}{
\p{One more correction to the table: when the home directory is in #{M} and receives a \code{GetS}, it must not clear the owner field as the figure directs. Once in #{S^D}, the directory can no longer tell whether an incoming \code{PutM} came from the owner (and should stall) or from a stale node. The owner is only cleared once the data has actually arrived.}
}
